<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna `unmergecall` (default off).  gcc -Os cross-jumps the tails of
    paths that end in the same call: each path sets the arguments and jumps
    to one shared `call f` block, so the decompiler sees merged arguments
    and prints `if (c <= 5) { b2 = 2; a = b; } return f(a, b2) + 3;`.  With
    the option on, the shared block is split per predecessor and each path
    gets its own call: `if (6 <= c) return f(a,1) + 3; return f(b,2) + 3;`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P8 "goto-quality" (ActionUnmergeCall, group
    returnsplit).

    Source (gcc -Os -fno-pie -no-pie -nostartfiles):
      __attribute__((noinline)) int f(int x, int y) { return x * 31 + y; }
      int pick(int c, int a, int b) { int r; if (c > 5) r = f(a, 1); else r = f(b, 2); return r + 3; }
      int pick3(int c, int a, int b) { if (c == 1) return f(a, 7) * 2; if (c == 2) return f(b, 7) * 2; return f(a + b, 9) * 2; }

    Pass 1 is the default (merged arguments); pass 2 turns the option on.
-->
